# Prospektor

> Prospektor tells a B2B sales team which companies to approach, and what to
> send them. The second half is the product: a deck, a one-pager, emails and a
> proposal written in one client's voice, grounded in the work that client has
> already won.

This domain is the marketing site: what the product is, and what it costs. The
application is `studio.prospektor.ai`, and it has its own file for machines —
[studio.prospektor.ai/llms.txt](https://studio.prospektor.ai/llms.txt) lists the
calls that need no account. Nothing on this domain is an API.

## The price

US$999 a month, per workspace, paid before you start. A workspace is one company
you are selling for, so an agency selling for five clients keeps five. Runs are
unlimited and every colleague on your work-email domain is included — no seats to
count, no credit meter. Money back if it doesn't earn its keep.
[prospektor.ai/pricing/](https://prospektor.ai/pricing/) answers the rest of the
money questions.

## What it does

**Who to pitch** — it reads a company's own site, scores fit against your brief,
and names the same-named companies it ruled out. **What to send** — the deck, the
one-pager, the emails and the proposal, in your voice, grounded in work you have
already won. The second half is the hard one, and it is what you are paying for.

Five agents do the run, and each one shows its work:

- **BDR** — brings companies worth pitching, scored against your brief.
- **SDR** — decision-makers with a source behind every name, then who in your network can introduce you.
- **AE** — writes the deck, the one-pager, the proposal and the emails.
- **Sales Ops** — checks the work before you see it: an invented number, a fabricated person, a ruled-out company.
- **RevOps** — remembers what worked, so run 100 is not run 1.

They research, draft and check. You send — nothing signs your name but you. The
two halves in full: [prospektor.ai/who-to-pitch/](https://prospektor.ai/who-to-pitch/)
and [prospektor.ai/what-to-send/](https://prospektor.ai/what-to-send/).

